## Consus separates sensitive inference payloads from billing and audit metadata

Source: Hacker News · Eric / Consus — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50073471
Published: 2026-10-13
Channel: hn

Consus founder Eric introduced an OpenAI-compatible gateway for organizations handling sensitive data. The announcement says keys and policy are checked before inference, model names require a compliance suffix, and ordinary commercial model endpoints are not used. It also describes per-user model restrictions and spend controls. Most relevant to observability, the founder says prompts and completions remain only in memory for the request while billing and audit metadata are retained. The claimed compliance posture and retention behavior have not been independently assessed by this publication.

That separation changes what an incident investigator can recover. Our editorial recommendation is to define the metadata required to connect caller identity, policy decision, selected model, timing, and outcome without storing sensitive content. Test failure paths and logging middleware for accidental payload capture, and distinguish evidence of an authorized request from evidence of a correct answer. Teams may need privacy-preserving application feedback or separately governed evaluation fixtures to investigate quality failures. A gateway audit trail and a full conversational replay answer different operational questions.

Why it matters: Specify the evidence needed for debugging before choosing a payload-retention policy.

