## Langfuse joins traces into a continuous conversation for debugging

Source: Langfuse — https://langfuse.com/changelog/2026-10-13-redesigned-session-view
Published: 2026-10-13
Channel: news

Langfuse announced a redesigned session reader that combines user and assistant messages across traces into one continuous conversation. A sidebar connects messages, individual traces, and tool usage, allowing a reviewer to move from a problematic exchange to the corresponding execution record. The sessions table also gains filters distinguishing available tools from called tools, plus a tool-call count filter. The announcement recommends a shared sessionId for observations belonging to the same interaction. It describes a viewing and investigation feature, rather than a new guarantee of complete instrumentation.

For multi-turn agents, the useful improvement is continuity: an apparently reasonable response can be wrong in light of an earlier request or a tool result. Our editorial recommendation is to verify that separate model calls, application turns, and retries retain the intended session identity. Test a conversation containing a failed tool call and confirm that the viewer links it to the response that followed. Also check parallel sessions for accidental merging. A clearer reader can expose missing context, but it cannot reconstruct events that the application never recorded.

Why it matters: Verify session identity and tool coverage so the improved conversation reader has trustworthy inputs.

