## Langfuse opens its System One evaluator adapter to custom model endpoints

Source: Langfuse — https://langfuse.com/changelog/2026-10-13-system-one-compatible-decision-models
Published: 2026-10-13
Channel: news

Langfuse expanded its TypeSafe System One connection to models serving the same API, instead of limiting the adapter to Jev. Users select a custom upstream and supply a base URL and model name; Choice, Score, and Yes/no questions continue to use the existing question types. The announcement names SubQ Snap and a self-hosted Strands Decider as supported examples. Suggested uses include routing traces by failure category, flagging sensitive outputs, and scoring answer completeness. The post does not establish that different evaluator models produce equivalent judgments.

A compatible interface makes evaluator substitution easier, but score meaning still depends on the model, rubric, and input distribution. Our editorial recommendation is to replay a versioned set of representative examples before switching an upstream, compare disagreements with human labels, and record the evaluator identity alongside each result. Include cases with incomplete context and ambiguous evidence. Track evaluator cost and latency separately from the application being evaluated. Otherwise a cheaper or locally hosted judge can change the interpretation of a production quality trend without an obvious schema change.

Why it matters: Treat a change of evaluator model as a measurement change and calibrate it against labeled examples.

