## Arize separates prompt tone from brevity in a six-model evaluation

Source: Arize AI · Jim Bennett — https://arize.com/blog/does-being-rude-to-ai-work/
Published: 2026-10-13
Channel: news

Jim Bennett reports a six-model experiment investigating whether hostile prompts improve answers or reduce token use. The initial comparison mixed tone with instructions asking for shorter responses. Removing those brevity instructions changed the apparent savings, while isolated tone did not show a consistent accuracy advantage. The article records correctness, output tokens, hidden thinking tokens, and latency, and warns that shorter visible answers can coincide with more hidden reasoning. These are the author’s experimental findings on the chosen tasks, not a universal result for every deployed model or workflow.

For an evaluation program, the broader lesson is experimental control. Our editorial recommendation is to vary one prompt factor at a time, preserve the task and scoring procedure, and report the full cost of a successful outcome rather than visible output length alone. Include representative user frustration in test data without assuming abusive wording is a useful optimization. Inspect model-specific regressions and interaction effects, then repeat the comparison on actual product tasks. A dramatic token chart can be explained by a changed instruction rather than the behavioral hypothesis it appears to support.

Why it matters: Separate tone from instructions and measure hidden reasoning before attributing a cost improvement to a prompt change.

