## Elastic demonstrates how to verify retention and query old audit evidence

Source: Elastic Observability Labs · Jeffrey Rengifo — https://www.elastic.co/observability-labs/blog/audit-logging-retention-elasticsearch
Published: 2026-10-13
Channel: news

Elastic’s audit-logging walkthrough follows a small fixture through the EDOT Collector into a dedicated Elasticsearch data stream. It reconstructs an actor timeline with ES|QL, reads back the applied retention configuration, and repeats the query against a mounted searchable snapshot. The article distinguishes audit evidence from ordinary application logs and warns that configuration read-back does not prove a year of availability. Its lab does not establish cold-tier cost or latency benchmarks, and a read-only index is not protection against every privileged administrator.

For teams operating agents that can change external systems, our editorial recommendation is to connect the agent’s execution trace with a separate record of who authorized an action, what target changed, and what outcome the provider returned. Rehearse retrieval of older evidence and check source coverage rather than treating a retention setting as proof of completeness. Separate investigation access from retention administration, and avoid retaining sensitive conversational content by default when action metadata is sufficient. The useful standard is evidence that can still answer an incident question months later, within the organization’s actual data policy.

Why it matters: Rehearse evidence retrieval and check source coverage; retention configuration alone does not prove an auditable history.

