## Dynatrace compares Collector builds by component coverage and operational ownership

Source: Dynatrace · Amrith Raj — https://www.dynatrace.com/news/blog/opentelemetry-collector-distributions-compared-core-contrib-ocb-and-vendor-builds-which-one-should-you-use/
Published: 2026-10-13
Channel: news

Dynatrace's Amrith Raj compares four OpenTelemetry Collector choices: lean Core, broad Contrib, custom distributions assembled with the Collector Builder (OCB), and vendor packages. The key distinction is which components a binary includes and who owns its build, upgrades, and support. Contrib components have maturity labels that can vary by telemetry signal. A custom OCB build selects and pins modules; vendor packages curate their own set, with support and testing dependent on the offering. The examples are illustrative, so component names and versions need verification before deployment.

Our editorial connection is the transport layer beneath AI tracing. Before choosing a package, write down the receivers, processors, exporters, and signals your agent workloads require. In a staging environment, test whether model and tool-call attributes survive the pipeline, whether sensitive fields are removed as intended, and whether telemetry still arrives after an upgrade. Compare those requirements with the chosen release's actual component manifest. A distribution label alone does not establish trace completeness or privacy protection; those outcomes need evidence from the running pipeline.

Why it matters: Choose against a tested AI telemetry pipeline and an explicit ownership model.

